---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import NavBar from '../components/NavBar.astro';
import Diagram from '../components/Diagram.astro';
import { site } from '../data/site';
---

<BaseLayout title="TUI" description="amber terminal UI, BitchX-inspired IRC-style multi-window agent interface for Linux and macOS">
  <NavBar />
  <main>
    <h1>The TUI</h1>

    <section class="section">
      <p>
        Amber's terminal UI is a BitchX/IRC-inspired multi-window interface for
        interacting with the agent directly from the terminal. It runs on
        Linux and macOS.
      </p>
    </section>

    <hr class="divider-thick" />

    <section class="section">
      <h2>Status Bar</h2>
      <p>The status bar sits at the bottom of the screen, always visible:</p>
      <div class="terminal">
        <pre>───── amber {site.version} ───── {site.license} ───── C++17 ───── utf-8 ───── ncurses ─────</pre>
      </div>
      <p>Connection state indicator, version number, and current section. Static but present.</p>
    </section>

    <hr class="divider" />

    <section class="section">
      <h2>Slash Commands</h2>
      <p>
        Type <code>/</code> to open the command drawer. Commands are JSON-driven, no hardcoded
        completions. The tree structure supports unlimited nesting:
      </p>
      <Diagram
        name="commandTreeStructure"
      />
      <Diagram
        name="commandTreeDispatch"
      />
      <div class="terminal">
        <pre>/set model              # switch models
/set provider           # switch providers
/get model list         # list available models
/get plugin             # list plugins and their state
/set plugin &lt;id&gt; on     # enable a plugin
/set policy rule        # manage permission rules
/get job status         # check running jobs</pre>
      </div>
    </section>

    <hr class="divider" />

    <section class="section">
      <h2>Command Drawer</h2>
      <p>
        The drawer shows available commands with short help text. It's not a modal dialog -
        it's a terminal drawer that slides up from the bottom, like BitchX's command list.
      </p>
      <div class="terminal">
        <pre>─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  /set model           Switch LLM model
  /set provider        Switch LLM provider
  /get model list      List available models
  /get plugin          List plugins and their state
  /get job status      Check running jobs
  /set policy rule     Manage permission rules
─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</pre>
      </div>
      <p>
        Every row above comes from the tree. Runtime values, models,
        providers, policy rules, jobs, plugins, MCP servers, are merged in as
        feed leaves, so nothing a user can type is hardcoded in C++:
      </p>
      <Diagram
        name="commandFeeds"
      />
    </section>

    <hr class="divider" />

    <section class="section">
      <h2>Sessions & Jobs</h2>
      <p>
        Multiple sessions can run concurrently, each with its own context and
        provider. Background jobs are tracked and can be inspected or killed
        from the command tree.
      </p>
      <div class="terminal">
        <pre>/get job status         # check running jobs
/get job &lt;id&gt; read     # read a job's output
/get job &lt;id&gt; kill     # kill a job</pre>
      </div>
    </section>

    <hr class="divider" />

    <section class="section">
      <h2>Plugin Controls</h2>
      <p>
        Plugins can be enabled and disabled at runtime. The command tree
        reflects the current plugin state:
      </p>
      <div class="terminal">
        <pre>/get plugin             # list all plugins and their state
/set plugin anthropic on   # enable the Anthropic provider
/set plugin anthropic off  # disable (unwinds cleanly)</pre>
      </div>
    </section>

    <hr class="divider" />

    <section class="section">
      <h2>Tool Call Visibility</h2>
      <p>
        You don't just see what the agent says, you see what it's doing. Tool calls are
        displayed as they execute, with output capping and timeout enforcement.
      </p>
      <div class="terminal">
        <pre>[tool] read_file: /home/jack/projects/readme.md
  → 42 lines read, 1.2KB
[tool] search_files: pattern="TODO" in /home/jack/projects/
  → 12 matches found
[tool] bash: grep -rnI "fixme" /home/jack/projects/
  → 3 matches in 2 files</pre>
      </div>
    </section>

    <hr class="divider" />

    <section class="section">
      <h2>Compression Status</h2>
      <p>
        When the compression pipeline runs, you see live progress, not a spinner, actual
        status updates as the pipeline classifies, extracts, and prunes:
      </p>
      <div class="terminal">
        <pre>[compress] Classify: 248 messages → core: 12, context: 18, prune: 218
[compress] Extract: 3 memory ops, 2 skill ops
[compress] Prune: replaced 218 bulky outputs with placeholders
[compress] Headroom: 25% enforced (186/736 tokens)
[compress] Done: 248 → 30 messages (87.9% reduction)</pre>
      </div>
    </section>

    <hr class="divider" />

    <section class="section">
      <h2>Approval Dialogs</h2>
      <p>
        The agent asks before running shell commands. Approval dialogs appear as modal prompts
        with the command text, workspace path, and a yes/no choice:
      </p>
      <div class="terminal">
        <pre>─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Approval required: bash tool

  Command: rm -rf /home/jack/projects/build
  Workspace: /home/jack/projects
  Timeout: 60s

  [Y]es  [N]o  [A]lways allow  [E]xit
─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────</pre>
      </div>
    </section>

    <hr class="divider" />

    <section class="section">
      <h2>Multi-Window Chat</h2>
      <p>
        Like BitchX, amber supports multiple chat windows. Each window can connect to a
        different LLM or run a different agent task. Window switching is
        <code>Alt+1</code> through <code>Alt+9</code>, the same muscle memory
        as the good old days of IRC.
      </p>
    </section>

    <hr class="divider" />

    <section class="section">
      <h2>Runtime Telemetry</h2>
      <p>
        The status bar is composed from a plugin registry, surfacing runtime
        state, connection, model, provider, job count, and metrics from the
        bundled metrics plugin, without hardcoding UI segments.
      </p>
    </section>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
